    sources: [ // every label says what the page says and when it was read (sweep 7A)
      { label: "IETF, RFC 4949, Internet Security Glossary, Version 2, R. Shirey, August 2007 (read 2026-11-12): a man-in-the-middle attack is a form of active wiretapping in which the attacker intercepts and selectively modifies communicated data to masquerade as one or more of the entities involved; the Diffie-Hellman-Merkle example without data origin authentication, separate session keys with each party, a clandestine proxy", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4949" }, // IETF
      { label: "NIST CSRC Glossary, man-in-the-middle attack (MitM) (read 2026-11-12): an attacker positioned between two communicating parties to intercept and/or alter data; in authentication, between claimant and verifier, registrant and CSP during enrolment, or subscriber and CSP during authenticator binding (NIST SP 1800-21B, under Person (Man)-in-the-Middle Attack); abbreviations MitM and MITM; NISTIR 7711", href: "https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/man_in_the_middle_attack" }, // NIST CSRC Glossary
      { label: "MITRE ATT&CK, T1557 Adversary-in-the-Middle, version 2.5, created 11 February 2020, last modified 3 November 2026 (read 2026-11-12): abusing ARP, DNS, LLMNR and similar protocols to force traffic through an adversary-controlled system; interception of credentials, access tokens and session cookies; downgrade attacks; four sub-techniques; mitigations including encryption, disabling legacy protocols, segmentation and user training on certificate errors", href: "https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557/" }, // MITRE ATT&CK
      { label: "MITRE ATT&CK, T1557.002 ARP Cache Poisoning (read 2026-11-12): replying faster than the legitimate owner or sending gratuitous ARP replies; ARP is stateless and does not require authentication; mitigations: DHCP snooping and Dynamic ARP Inspection on switches, static ARP entries, ignoring gratuitous replies", href: "https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557/002/" }, // MITRE ATT&CK
      { label: "MITRE ATT&CK, T1557.001 Name Resolution Poisoning and SMB Relay (read 2026-11-12): spoofed answers to LLMNR (UDP 5355), NBT-NS (UDP 137) and mDNS (UDP 5353); the username and NTLMv2 hash sent to the adversary, cracked offline or relayed", href: "https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557/001/" }, // MITRE ATT&CK
      { label: "MITRE ATT&CK, T1557.003 DHCP Spoofing and T1557.004 Evil Twin (read 2026-11-12): a rogue DHCP server handing out adversary-owned DNS servers; DHCPv6 INFORMATION-REQUEST (code 11); seemingly genuine Wi-Fi access points using a legitimate SSID and a stronger signal", href: "https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557/003/" }, // MITRE ATT&CK
      { label: "IETF, RFC 3833, Threat Analysis of the Domain Name System (DNS), D. Atkins and R. Austein, August 2004 (read 2026-11-12): packet interception, monkey-in-the-middle attacks and spoofed responses that beat the real response back to the resolver; one unsigned, unencrypted UDP packet; a 16-bit ID, 2**32 combinations with the client port, often below 2**16; written because the threats DNSSEC protects against had never been written down", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3833" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 6797, HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), J. Hodges, C. Jackson and A. Barth, November 2012 (read 2026-11-12): click-through insecurity and leaked session cookies; the Strict-Transport-Security header field; the required max-age directive and includeSubDomains; errors treated as fatal with no user recourse; the bootstrap MITM vulnerability; pre-loaded lists", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6797" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 9525, Service Identity in TLS, P. Saint-Andre and R. Salz, November 2023 (read 2026-11-12): obsoletes RFC 6125; the Common Name RDN must not be used to identify a service", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9525" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 9162, Certificate Transparency Version 2.0, December 2021, Experimental (read 2026-11-12): obsoletes RFC 6962; publicly logging TLS server certificates so anyone can audit CA activity and notice suspect certificates", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9162" }, // IETF
      { label: "Fox-IT for the Dutch Ministry of the Interior and Kingdom Relations, Black Tulip: Report of the investigation into the DigiNotar Certificate Authority breach, 13 August 2012, published by the Dutch government (read 2026-11-12): breach on 17 June 2011, Secure-net reached on 1 July, first rogue certificate on 10 July; all eight CA servers compromised; 531 rogue certificates; the 28 August forum post and Chrome's pinning; 298,140 unique IP addresses in OCSP logs, 95% in Iran, 143 autonomous systems; trust revoked on 3 September; bankruptcy petition on 19 September and declaration on 20 September 2011", href: "https://zoek.officielebekendmakingen.nl/blg-191225.pdf" }, // Fox-IT for the Dutch Ministry of the Int
      { label: "Fortinet, FortiOS 7.6.5 Administration Guide, Deep inspection (read 2026-11-12): the FortiGate impersonates the recipient of the originating SSL session, decrypts, inspects and re-encrypts; SMTPS, POP3S, IMAPS and FTPS too; Fortinet_CA_SSL, Fortinet_CA_Untrusted or an uploaded CA; never import Fortinet_CA_Untrusted; exemptions by address, category or allowlist", href: "https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortigate/7.6.5/administration-guide/122078" }, // Fortinet
      { label: "F5, SSL Orchestrator Deployment Guide, 4.4 Managing Cryptography (read 2026-11-12): the client handshake paused at the Client Hello, the server certificate validated, a re-issued ('forged') certificate signed by a local CA the clients must trust; two separate TLS sessions; certificate pinning is solely a function of the client and defeats any decrypting intermediary; the Pinners bypass category", href: "https://clouddocs.f5.com/sslo-deployment-guide/chapter4/page4.4.html" }, // F5
    ], // 13 sources, one object per line
