    sources: [ // every label says what the page says and when it was read (sweep 10A+10C)
      { label: "NIST, SP 800-41 Rev. 1, Guidelines on Firewalls and Firewall Policy, K. Scarfone and P. Hoffman, September 2009, the current final per the CSRC publication page (read 2026-11-12): the packet filter as the most basic firewall feature; stateless inspection firewalls that do not track flows; rules on source and destination address, protocol, ports, interface and direction; routers with ACLs as the purest example; ingress and egress filtering; vulnerability to spoofing and IP source routing; fragments, VPN interoperability and reassembly costs; most packet-filtering firewalls keep some state", href: "https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/SP/nistspecialpublication800-41r1.pdf" }, // NIST
      { label: "NIST CSRC Glossary, packet filter (read 2026-11-12): a routing device that provides access control functionality for host addresses and communication sessions (source: SP 800-41 Rev. 1)", href: "https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/packet_filter" }, // NIST CSRC Glossary
      { label: "Jeffrey C. Mogul, Simple and Flexible Datagram Access Controls for Unix-based Gateways, DEC Western Research Laboratory Research Report 89/4, March 1989, preprint for the Summer 1989 USENIX conference, read from the bitsavers mirror (read 2026-11-12): screening the packets that flow through a Unix-based gateway; the kernel asks a user-level process, screend, to judge every packet to be forwarded; a table-driven configuration file", href: "https://ftp.mirrorservice.org/sites/www.bitsavers.org/pdf/dec/tech_reports/WRL-89-4.pdf" }, // Jeffrey C. Mogul
      { label: "Wikipedia, Firewall (computing), last edited 10 November 2026 (read 2026-11-12): the packet filter as the first reported type of network firewall; first paper by DEC engineers dated 1987; Cheswick and Bellovin at AT&T Bell Labs; circuit-level gateways from 1989 to 1990", href: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firewall_(computing)" }, // Wikipedia
      { label: "Steven McCanne and Van Jacobson, The BSD Packet Filter: A New Architecture for User-level Packet Capture, preprint dated 19 December 1992 for the Winter USENIX conference of 25 to 29 January 1993 (read 2026-11-12): the CMU/Stanford Packet Filter (CSPF) completed in 1980 from the Xerox Alto packet filter; BPF's register-based evaluator up to 20 times faster than the stack-based design, overall up to 100 times faster than Sun's NIT", href: "https://www.tcpdump.org/papers/bpf-usenix93.pdf" }, // Steven McCanne and Van Jacobson
      { label: "tcpdump and libpcap, pcap-filter(7) manual page (read 2026-11-12): the filter expression language for packet capture, such as ether proto, vlan and mpls primitives", href: "https://www.tcpdump.org/manpages/pcap-filter.7.html" }, // tcpdump and libpcap
      { label: "Cisco, Configure Commonly Used IP ACLs, Document ID 26448, updated 28 January 2025 (read 2026-11-12): an implicit deny all at the end of every ACL; standard ACLs match source addresses, extended ACLs addresses, protocol and ports; the established keyword matches TCP datagrams with the ACK or RST bit set, as in access-list 102 permit tcp any any gt 1023 established; reflexive and context-based ACLs listed among IOS ACL types", href: "https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/ip/access-lists/26448-ACLsamples.html" }, // Cisco
      { label: "IETF, RFC 8519, YANG Data Model for Network Access Control Lists (ACLs), M. Jethanandani et al., March 2019, Standards Track (read 2026-11-12): actions on the first matching ACE are applied with no processing of subsequent ACEs", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8519.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "OpenBSD, pf.conf(5) manual page, dated 18 January 2026 (read 2026-11-12): rules evaluated from first to last, the last matching rule decides unless quick is set; with no match the packet passes without creating state; pf filters statefully by default, with flags S/SA for stateful TCP unless no state is given", href: "https://man.openbsd.org/pf.conf.5" }, // OpenBSD
      { label: "nftables wiki, Matching connection tracking stateful metainformation, last edited 15 April 2021 (read 2026-11-12): ct state new, established, related, invalid and untracked; a simple stateful firewall with policy drop and ct state established,related accept", href: "https://wiki.nftables.org/wiki-nftables/index.php/Matching_connection_tracking_stateful_metainformation" }, // nftables wiki
      { label: "F5, BIG-IP TMOS: Routing Administration 14.1.0, Packet Filters (read 2026-11-12): packet filters apply to incoming traffic only; disabled by default; Unhandled Packet Action Accept (default), Discard or Reject; Filter established connections off by default; rule order First, Last or After; expressions in tcpdump syntax; ICMP type 3 code 13 on reject when enabled", href: "https://techdocs.f5.com/en-us/bigip-14-1-0/big-ip-tmos-routing-administration-14-1-0/packet-filters.html" }, // F5
      { label: "Extreme Networks, Switch Engine v34.1.1 User Guide, ACLs Overview and ACL Rule Syntax, 1 November 2026 (read 2026-11-12): ACLs filter packets on ingress, and on egress on some platforms, with no impact on switch performance; they apply to all traffic, so denying all traffic on a port drops OSPF or RIP adjacencies; rule entries with match conditions and an action, permitted by default when no action is given", href: "https://documentation.extremenetworks.com/Switch%20Engine%20v34.1.1%20User%20Guide/content/documents/Switch_Operating_Systems/Switch_Engine/User_Guide/acls_overview.shtml" }, // Extreme Networks
      { label: "IETF, RFC 1858, Security Considerations for IP Fragment Filtering, G. Ziemba, D. Reed and P. Traina, October 1995, Informational (read 2026-11-12): the tiny fragment attack, which pushes TCP header fields into the second fragment so that filter rules do not match; the 8-octet minimum fragment is not enough; the direct method (drop zero-offset fragments with a transport header shorter than TMIN) and the indirect method (drop TCP fragments with offset 1); the overlapping fragment attack", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1858.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 3128, Protection Against a Variant of the Tiny Fragment Attack, I. Miller, June 2001, Informational, updates RFC 1858 (read 2026-11-12): RFC 1858 compliant filters can be vulnerable to a variant of the tiny fragment attack", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3128.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 2827 (BCP 38), Network Ingress Filtering: Defeating Denial of Service Attacks which employ IP Source Address Spoofing, P. Ferguson and D. Senie, May 2000, obsoletes RFC 2267 (read 2026-11-12): ingress filtering at the network edge so that traffic with forged source addresses is dropped", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2827.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 1191, Path MTU Discovery, J. Mogul and S. Deering, November 1990 (read 2026-11-12): the source host learns the path MTU from ICMP Destination Unreachable messages with the code meaning fragmentation needed and DF set", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1191.txt" }, // IETF
    ], // 16 sources, one object per line
