    sources: [ // every label says what the page says and when it was read (batch 14A)
      { label: "Python 3.13 documentation, ipaddress: IPv4/IPv6 manipulation library (read 2026-11-12): a host mask is the logical inverse of a net mask, sometimes used (for example in Cisco access control lists) to denote a network mask, 0.0.0.255 for /24; a mask after the slash read as a net mask if its first field is non-zero and as a host mask if it is zero, an all-zero mask being a net mask; 192.168.1.0/24, 192.168.1.0/255.255.255.0 and 192.168.1.0/0.0.0.255 equivalent; the hostmask and with_hostmask attributes", href: "https://docs.python.org/3.13/library/ipaddress.html" }, // Python 3.13 documentation
      { label: "Cisco, Configure IP Access Lists, Document ID 23602, updated 15 October 2025 (read 2026-11-12): ACLs use a wildcard mask, also called an inverse mask; a 0 bit must match and a 1 bit is ignored; the inverse mask is 255.255.255.255 minus the subnet mask; 0.0.0.255, 0.0.7.255 for 255.255.248.0 and 0.0.1.255 examples; host as a 0.0.0.0 wildcard, any as 0.0.0.0 with 255.255.255.255; an omitted wildcard in a standard ACL taken as all zeros; the implicit deny", href: "https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/security/ios-firewall/23602-confaccesslists.html" }, // Cisco
      { label: "Cisco, ASA 5500 Series Configuration Guide using the CLI 8.3, Adding an Extended Access List (read 2026-11-12): the ASA uses a network mask such as 255.255.255.0 in access lists, unlike the Cisco IOS access-list command, whose mask uses wildcard bits such as 0.0.0.255", href: "https://www3-realm.cisco.com/en/US/docs/security/asa/asa83/configuration/guide/acl_extended.html" }, // Cisco
      { label: "Ivan Pepelnjak, Be Smart When Using the OSPF Network Statement, blog post, 12 July 2007 (read 2026-11-12): the wildcard bits of the OSPF network statement need not be the inverse of the interface mask; network statements work like a simple IP access list, sorted from most to least specific, first match wins; network x.y.z.w 0.0.0.0, network 10.1.16.0 0.0.15.255 area 3 and network 0.0.0.0 255.255.255.255 as patterns", href: "https://blog.ipspace.net/2007/07/be-smart-when-using-ospf-network/" }, // Ivan Pepelnjak
      { label: "Wikipedia, Wildcard mask (read 2026-11-12): used in Cisco IOS for OSPF network size and for ACLs; 0 must match and 1 does not matter; any bit pattern possible, 0.0.0.254 matching even addresses with 10.10.10.2 and odd ones with 10.10.10.1; the table of prefix lengths, netmasks and wildcard masks", href: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wildcard_mask" }, // Wikipedia
      { label: "Tony Mattke, Using discontiguous wildcard masks in ACLs, Router Jockey, 19 May 2010 (read 2026-11-12): unlike subnet masks, wildcard masks allow discontiguous bits; their use in routing protocols frowned upon if not prohibited; the more complex the mask, the more unintended traffic it matches", href: "https://routerjockey.com/using-discontiguous-wildcard-masks-in-acls/" }, // Tony Mattke
      { label: "Fortinet, FortiOS 7.2.4 CLI Reference, config router access-list (read 2026-11-12): each rule has a prefix for regular filter criteria or a wildcard to define Cisco-style wildcard filter criteria, with exact-match", href: "https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortigate/7.2.4/cli-reference/566620" }, // Fortinet
      { label: "iptables(8), Linux manual page (read 2026-11-12): the mask after the slash is an IPv4 network mask or a plain number of leading ones, so a mask of 24 equals 255.255.255.0", href: "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/iptables.8.html" }, // iptables(8)
    ], // 8 sources, one object per line
