    sources: [ // every label says what the page says and when it was read (sweep 9D)
      { label: "US Department of Defense, MIL-STD-1629A, Procedures for Performing a Failure Mode, Effects and Criticality Analysis, 24 November 1980, copy hosted by NASA Kennedy Space Center (read 2026-11-12): section 3.1.19, single failure point: the failure of an item which would result in failure of the system and is not compensated for by redundancy or alternative operational procedure; section 4.5.2.2 requires a separate list of all single failure points, and the final report names those that could not be eliminated from the design", href: "https://extapps.ksc.nasa.gov/reliability/Documents/milstd1629_FMEA.pdf" }, // US Department of Defense
      { label: "NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical Methods, section 8.1.8.2, Series model (read 2026-11-12): the system fails when the first component fails; with independent components the reliabilities multiply and the failure rates add", href: "https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/apr/section1/apr182.htm" }, // NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical 
      { label: "NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical Methods, section 8.1.8.3, Parallel or redundant model (read 2026-11-12): the system works as long as at least one component works; with independent components the system's failure probability is the product of the components' failure probabilities", href: "https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/apr/section1/apr183.htm" }, // NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical 
      { label: "IETF, RFC 9568, Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol (VRRP) Version 3 for IPv4 and IPv6, April 2024, obsoletes RFC 5798 (read 2026-11-12): a manually configured default route creates a single point of failure; loss of the default router isolates all end-hosts; VRRP is designed to eliminate it by electing an Active Router", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9568.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 2182 (BCP 16), Selection and Operation of Secondary DNS Servers, July 1997 (read 2026-11-12): the DNS requires multiple servers for every delegated zone; secondary servers must be topologically and geographically dispersed to minimize the likelihood of a single failure disabling all of them; servers on the same LAN segment in the same room almost defeat the requirement", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2182.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 4202, Routing Extensions in Support of Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS), October 2005 (read 2026-11-12): section 2.3, a shared risk link group (SRLG) is a set of links that share a resource whose failure may affect all of them, such as two fibres in the same conduit; identified by a 32-bit number; diversely routed paths should have disjoint SRLGs", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4202.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "Amazon Web Services, AWS Well-Architected Framework, Reliability Pillar, Design principles (read 2026-11-12): scale horizontally, replacing one large resource with multiple small ones and distributing requests so that they do not share a common point of failure", href: "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/reliability-pillar/design-principles.html" }, // Amazon Web Services
      { label: "Cloudflare (Matthew Prince), Post mortem on the Cloudflare Control Plane and Analytics Outage, 4 November 2023 (read 2026-11-12): three independent data centres around Hillsboro, Oregon; both utility feeds and all 10 generators of PDX-04 offline after a ground fault; batteries expected to last about 10 minutes failed after 4; the facility's access control system was not on battery backup; services in the high availability cluster depended on Kafka and ClickHouse running only in PDX-04; the whole facility had never been taken offline in a test; logging left out of the cluster on purpose", href: "https://blog.cloudflare.com/post-mortem-on-cloudflare-control-plane-and-analytics-outage/" }, // Cloudflare (Matthew Prince)
      { label: "Meta Engineering (Santosh Janardhan), More details about the October 4 outage, 5 October 2021 (read 2026-11-12): a maintenance command took down the whole backbone after a bug in the audit tool failed to stop it; DNS servers withdrew their BGP advertisements when they could not reach the data centres; the loss of DNS broke internal tools; primary and out-of-band access were down; storm drills", href: "https://engineering.fb.com/2021/10/05/networking-traffic/outage-details/" }, // Meta Engineering (Santosh Janardhan)
      { label: "Fastly (Nick Rockwell), Summary of June 8 outage, 8 June 2021 (read 2026-11-12): a bug introduced by a software deployment begun on 12 May was triggered by a valid customer configuration change; 85% of the network returned errors; detected within one minute; 95% of the network normal within 49 minutes", href: "https://www.fastly.com/blog/summary-of-june-8-outage" }, // Fastly (Nick Rockwell)
      { label: "CrowdStrike, Falcon Content Update Preliminary Post Incident Report, July 2024 (read 2026-11-12): a Rapid Response Content configuration update released on 19 July 2024 at 04:09 UTC crashed Windows hosts running sensor version 7.11 and above; reverted at 05:27 UTC; Mac and Linux hosts not affected", href: "https://www.crowdstrike.com/en-us/blog/falcon-content-update-preliminary-post-incident-report/" }, // CrowdStrike
      { label: "Microsoft (The Official Microsoft Blog), Helping our customers through the CrowdStrike outage, 20 July 2024 (read 2026-11-12): an estimated 8.5 million Windows devices affected, less than one per cent of all Windows machines", href: "https://blogs.microsoft.com/blog/2024/07/20/helping-our-customers-through-the-crowdstrike-outage/" }, // Microsoft (The Official Microsoft Blog)
      { label: "Principles of Chaos Engineering, last update March 2019 (read 2026-11-12): experimenting on a system to build confidence in its capability to withstand turbulent conditions; weaknesses to find include cascading failures when a single point of failure crashes; introduce real-world events such as servers that crash and network connections that are severed; minimize the blast radius", href: "https://principlesofchaos.org/" }, // Principles of Chaos Engineering
    ], // 13 sources, one object per line
