    sources: [ // every label says what the page says and when it was read (12/11/2026, batch 6A)
      { label: "The Jargon File, 'hacker': the term \"seems to have been first adopted as a badge in the 1960s by the hacker culture surrounding TMRC and the MIT AI Lab\", with a report of mid-1950s use \"by teenage radio hams and electronics tinkerers\" who were not at TMRC. The TMRC adoption is dated to the 1960s rather than the 1950s", href: "http://www.catb.org/jargon/html/H/hacker.html" }, // The Jargon File
      { label: "The Jargon File, version 4.2.2 of 20 August 2000, edited by Eric S. Raymond and Guy L. Steele, Project Gutenberg eBook 3008 (read 2026-11-12): hack in nine senses, from a quick job that produces what is needed but not well to an incredibly good piece of work, a prank and MIT roof and tunnel exploration; hacker in eight senses, the eighth deprecated in favour of cracker; cracker coined about 1985 against journalistic misuse, after a failed attempt with worm in 1981 and 1982; the hacker ethic; kludge and kluge; the 1960s badge and the mid-1950s radio hams", href: "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3008/pg3008.txt" }, // The Jargon File
      { label: "Tech Model Railroad Club of MIT, Abridged Dictionary of the TMRC Language, derived from Pete Samson's 1959 original (read 2026-11-12): Hack, an article or project without constructive end, work undertaken on bad self-advice, an entropy booster, to produce or attempt to produce a hack; Hacker, one who hacks, or makes them", href: "https://tmrc.mit.edu/old/dictionary.html" }, // Tech Model Railroad Club of MIT
      { label: "Tech Model Railroad Club of MIT, TMRC: Hackers (read 2026-11-12): a hacker applies ingenuity to create a clever result, a hack, usually quick and inelegant, reaching the goal without changing the design of the system it is embedded in; used proudly at TMRC since the late 1950s; the club resents the use for illegal acts; Levy's Hackers (Anchor Press/Doubleday, 1984) opens with TMRC", href: "https://tmrc.mit.edu/old/hackers-ref.html" }, // Tech Model Railroad Club of MIT
      { label: "Tech Model Railroad Club of MIT, home and About pages (read 2026-11-12): one of the oldest student activities at MIT, founded in the 1946-1947 school year, in Building 20 until 1997 and N52 since; the birthplace of the term hack", href: "https://tmrc.mit.edu/" }, // Tech Model Railroad Club of MIT
      { label: "IETF, RFC 1983 (FYI 18), Internet Users' Glossary, G. Malkin, editor, August 1996, obsoleting RFC 1392 (read 2026-11-12): hacker, a person who delights in having an intimate understanding of the internal workings of a system, often misused in a pejorative context where cracker would be correct; cracker, an individual who attempts to access computer systems without authorization, often malicious, as opposed to hackers", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1983.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "MIT News, Museum stages Ultimate Hack Week as exhibit closes, 28 February 2001 (read 2026-11-12): the MIT Museum's Hall of Hacks, shown almost continuously for ten years; the police car on the Great Dome in May 1994; the 1982 Harvard-Yale weather balloon; the Journal of the Institute for Hacks, TomFoolery, and Pranks", href: "https://news.mit.edu/2001/hacks-0228" }, // MIT News
      { label: "Google Books, Steven Levy, Hackers: Heroes of the Computer Revolution, 25th Anniversary Edition, O'Reilly Media, 19 May 2010, 520 pages (read 2026-11-12): the original hackers from the late 1950s to the early 1980s, their shared values known as the hacker ethic, and MIT students finagling access to computers", href: "https://books.google.de/books?id=mShXzzKtpmEC" }, // Google Books
    ], // 8 sources, one object per line
    // A PRECISION CORRECTION to the old label, which said "from the 1950s". The Jargon
    // File dates the TMRC adoption to the 1960s; the mid-1950s report is about radio hams
    // who were not at TMRC. The body text names no decade and needed no change.
