    sources: [ // every label says what the page says and when it was read (batch 13D)
      { label: "NIST Computer Security Resource Center, Glossary, 'red team' (read 2026-11-12): CNSSI 4009-2022 definition, a group authorized and organized to emulate a potential adversary's attack or exploitation capabilities against an enterprise's security posture, to show the impact of successful attacks and what works for the defenders, the Blue Team; the glossary's CNSS page expands CNSS as the Committee on National Security Systems", href: "https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/red_team" }, // NIST Computer Security Resource Center
      { label: "NIST Computer Security Resource Center, Glossary, 'red team exercise' (read 2026-11-12): from SP 800-53 Rev. 5 and SP 1800-21B, an exercise reflecting real-world conditions, a simulated adversarial attempt to compromise organizational missions or business processes, giving a comprehensive assessment of security capabilities", href: "https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/red_team_exercise" }, // NIST Computer Security Resource Center
      { label: "The Strategy Bridge, 'Advocatus Diaboli: Reviewing Red Team', 23 March 2016 (read 2026-11-12): review of Micah Zenko, Red Team (Basic Books, 2015); the concept traced to the sixteenth-century advocatus diaboli; the term from Cold War war games in which red played the enemy against blue friendly forces", href: "https://thestrategybridge.org/the-bridge/2016/3/23/advocatus-diaboli-reviewing-red-team" }, // The Strategy Bridge
      { label: "Council on Foreign Relations, book guide to Micah Zenko, 'Red Team: How to Succeed by Thinking Like the Enemy' (read 2026-11-12): red teams as modern-day devil's advocates across military, security, intelligence and business; seventeen case studies; six best practices; findings ignored; the 2014 theft of over twenty million US government personnel records", href: "https://www.cfr.org/education/teach/book-guides/red-team-how-succeed-thinking-enemy" }, // Council on Foreign Relations
      { label: "European Central Bank, 'What is TIBER-EU?' (read 2026-11-12): threat intelligence-based ethical red-teaming; tests mimic real attackers' TTPs; not pass or fail; five teams (blue, threat intelligence provider, red-team testers, control team, TIBER cyber team); published May 2018, updated in 2024 to align with DORA's TLPT standards; adopting jurisdictions; purple-teaming guidance", href: "https://www.ecb.europa.eu/paym/cyber-resilience/tiber-eu/html/index.en.html" }, // European Central Bank
      { label: "Bank of England, 'CBEST Threat Intelligence-Led Assessments: implementation guide' (read 2026-11-12): part of the supervisory toolkit of the Bank, the PRA and the FCA since 2014; a targeted assessment of systemically important firms and FMIs, cross-jurisdictional with other regulators", href: "https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/financial-stability/operational-resilience-of-the-financial-sector/cbest-threat-intelligence-led-assessments-implementation-guide" }, // Bank of England
      { label: "UK NCSC, 'Building a SOC: detection practices' (read 2026-11-12): red team exercises as authorized, non-destructive simulated attacks whose output is a report; ideally the SOC detects all red team activity; purple team exercises improve the capability iteratively; risks of testing on production", href: "https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/collection/building-a-security-operations-centre/detection/detection-practices" }, // UK NCSC
      { label: "Blake E. Strom and others, 'MITRE ATT&CK: Design and Philosophy', March 2020 (read 2026-11-12): ATT&CK created for structured adversary emulation exercises in MITRE's FMX environment; the emulation team used it for scenarios and defenders for measuring progress; red team or adversary emulation tests as part of a coverage claim", href: "https://attack.mitre.org/docs/ATTACK_Design_and_Philosophy_March_2020.pdf" }, // Blake E. Strom and others
      { label: "TechTarget, 'Microsoft, Fortra get court order to disrupt Cobalt Strike', 7 April 2023 (read 2026-11-12): Cobalt Strike introduced in 2012 as an adversary simulation platform, acquired by Fortra (formerly HelpSystems) in 2020; cracked legacy copies used by criminals; a court order of 31 March 2023 from the US District Court for the Eastern District of New York", href: "https://www.techtarget.com/searchsecurity/news/365534903/Microsoft-Fortra-get-court-order-to-disrupt-Cobalt-Strike" }, // TechTarget
      { label: "F5 Labs, Sander Vinberg, '2021 Credential Stuffing Report' (read 2026-11-12): written in February 2021 with the industry reeling from the theft of FireEye's red team tools and the SolarWinds Orion supply chain attack", href: "https://www.f5.com/labs/articles/2021-credential-stuffing-report" }, // F5 Labs
      { label: "FireEye (now Mandiant), 'Red Team Tool Countermeasures', README (read 2026-11-12): free detection rules for the stolen tools in Snort, YARA, ClamAV and HXIOC formats, released as production and supplemental rules", href: "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mandiant/red_team_tool_countermeasures/master/README.md" }, // FireEye (now Mandiant)
    ], // 11 sources, one object per line
