    sources: [ // every label says what the page says and when it was read (sweep 8C)
      { label: "NIST Computer Security Resource Center, Glossary, 'zero-day attack', from CNSSI 4009-2022 and NISTIR 8011 Vol. 3 (read 2026-11-12): 'An attack that exploits a previously unknown hardware, firmware, or software vulnerability'", href: "https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/zero_day_attack" }, // NIST Computer Security Resource Center
      { label: "Kim Zetter, 'Hacker Lexicon: What Is a Zero Day?', Wired, 11 November 2014 (read 2026-11-12): the term covers both the vulnerability and the exploit; it counts the days the vendor has known of the hole and apparently originated on bulletin board systems, where it counted the days since a program's release; black, white and grey markets and prices from $5,000 to several hundred thousand dollars; Stuxnet's five zero-day exploits, four at the time of discovery; Aurora and the RSA intrusion", href: "https://www.wired.com/2014/11/what-is-a-zero-day/" }, // Kim Zetter
      { label: "Google Threat Intelligence Group, 2025 zero-day review, 5 March 2026 (read 2026-11-12): 90 zero-days exploited in the wild in 2025, against 78 in 2024 and a record 100 in 2023, within a 60 to 100 range; GTIG's definition (exploited in the wild before a patch was publicly available); 43 (48 per cent) in enterprise technologies, 21 in security and networking products; more zero-days attributed to commercial surveillance vendors than to state-sponsored espionage groups for the first time; at least 10 by PRC-nexus groups; nine by financially motivated groups; all have patches available", href: "https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/2025-zero-day-review/" }, // Google Threat Intelligence Group
      { label: "Google Threat Intelligence Group, 'Hello 0-Days, My Old Friend: A 2024 Zero-Day Exploitation Analysis', 29 April 2025 (read 2026-11-12): the counts as first published (75 in 2024, 98 in 2023, 63 in 2022); 2024 targets including Ivanti Cloud Services Appliance, Palo Alto Networks PAN-OS, Cisco Adaptive Security Appliance and Ivanti Connect Secure VPN; security and network products as high-permission targets", href: "https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/2024-zero-day-trends" }, // Google Threat Intelligence Group
      { label: "Mandiant (Casey Charrier, Robert Weiner), 'How Low Can You Go? An Analysis of 2023 Time-to-Exploit Trends', 15 October 2024 (read 2026-11-12): 138 vulnerabilities disclosed in 2023 and exploited, 97 as zero-days and 41 as n-days (a 70:30 ratio, from 62:38 in 2021 and 2022); average time-to-exploit of 63 days in 2018 and 2019, 44, 32 and then five days in 2023; n-days exploited within one day (12 per cent), one week (29 per cent) and one month (56 per cent) of the patch; exploits as the top initial infection vector in its investigations from 2020 to 2023", href: "https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/time-to-exploit-trends-2023" }, // Mandiant (Casey Charrier
      { label: "Lillian Ablon and Andy Bogart, 'Zero Days, Thousands of Nights: The Life and Times of Zero-Day Vulnerabilities and Their Exploits', RAND Corporation, report RR-1751-RC, 9 March 2017 (read 2026-11-12): average life expectancy of 6.9 years; 25 per cent do not survive 1.51 years and 25 per cent live more than 9.5 years; about 5.7 per cent of a stockpile publicly discovered by another party after a year; a median of 22 days to develop a working exploit", href: "https://www.rand.org/pubs/research_reports/RR1751.html" }, // Lillian Ablon and Andy Bogart
      { label: "Google Project Zero, Vulnerability Disclosure Policy (read 2026-11-12): the 90+30 policy, a 14-day grace period, and a 7-day deadline with a 3-day grace period for vulnerabilities being actively exploited in the wild", href: "https://googleprojectzero.blogspot.com/p/vulnerability-disclosure-policy.html" }, // Google Project Zero
      { label: "Zero Day Initiative, Disclosure Policy (read 2026-11-12): vendors are allowed four months (120 days) to address a reported vulnerability, after which a limited advisory with mitigations may be published", href: "https://www.zerodayinitiative.com/advisories/disclosure_policy/" }, // Zero Day Initiative
      { label: "The White House, 'Vulnerabilities Equities Policy and Process for the United States Government', 15 November 2017 (read 2026-11-12): weighing dissemination to the vendor against temporarily restricting knowledge of newly discovered and not publicly known vulnerabilities; the Equities Review Board; 'in the vast majority of cases, responsibly disclosing a newly discovered vulnerability is clearly in the national interest'", href: "https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/sites/whitehouse.gov/files/images/External%20-%20Unclassified%20VEP%20Charter%20FINAL.PDF" }, // The White House
      { label: "Brad Smith, Microsoft, 'The need for urgent collective action to keep people safe online: Lessons from last week's cyberattack', 14 May 2017 (read 2026-11-12): the WannaCrypt exploits were drawn from exploits stolen from the NSA; Microsoft's patch of 14 March 2017; government stockpiling of vulnerabilities and the Tomahawk comparison", href: "https://blogs.microsoft.com/on-the-issues/2017/05/14/need-urgent-collective-action-keep-people-safe-online-lessons-last-weeks-cyberattack/" }, // Brad Smith
      { label: "Symantec Security Response, W32.Stuxnet Dossier, version 1.4, February 2011 (read 2026-11-12): zero-day exploits among Stuxnet's components, including the MS10-061 print spooler vulnerability and two escalation-of-privilege vulnerabilities", href: "https://docs.broadcom.com/doc/security-response-w32-stuxnet-dossier-11-en" }, // Symantec Security Response
      { label: "CISA, Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalogue, JSON feed, catalogue version 2026.11.10 (read 2026-11-12): 1,779 entries, the earliest added on 3 November 2021; each with a required action and a due date; 361 flagged as known to be used in ransomware campaigns", href: "https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/feeds/known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json" }, // CISA
      { label: "Verizon, 2026 Data Breach Investigations Report, landing page (read 2026-11-12): 31 per cent of breaches now start with software vulnerabilities, ahead of stolen passwords as the top way in", href: "https://www.verizon.com/business/resources/reports/dbir/" }, // Verizon
      { label: "OWASP Foundation, Virtual Patching Best Practices (read 2026-11-12): a virtual patch is 'a security policy enforcement layer which prevents the exploitation of a known vulnerability'; the term was coined by intrusion prevention system vendors and is mostly used for web application firewalls; it reduces risk until a vendor-supplied patch is released", href: "https://owasp.org/www-community/Virtual_Patching_Best_Practices" }, // OWASP Foundation
    ], // 14 sources, one object per line
