    sources: [ // every label says what the page says and when it was read (11/11/2026, batch 2)
      { label: "IETF, RFC 4291, IP Version 6 Addressing Architecture, R. Hinden and S. Deering, February 2006 (read 2026-11-11): three address types, unicast as an identifier for a single interface, anycast and multicast; no broadcast addresses in IPv6, their function superseded by multicast; address type identified by the high-order bits (FF00::/8 multicast, FE80::/10 link-local unicast, everything else global unicast); anycast addresses taken from the unicast space and syntactically indistinguishable from it", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4291.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IANA, IPv6 Address Space registry, last updated 2025-10-23 (read 2026-11-11): 2000::/3 Global Unicast; IANA unicast assignments limited to 2000::/3; the rest of the global unicast space, about 85 per cent of the IPv6 address space, reserved; fc00::/7 Unique Local Unicast; fe80::/10 Link-Scoped Unicast", href: "https://www.iana.org/assignments/ipv6-address-space/ipv6-address-space.txt" }, // IANA
      { label: "IETF, RFC 9542 (BCP 141), IANA Considerations and IETF Protocol and Documentation Usage for IEEE 802 Parameters, April 2024 (read 2026-11-11): the M bit of the first octet, the group or multicast bit; zero means a unicast MAC address, one means groupcast (multicast or broadcast); IANA OUI 00-00-5E with 2^24 unicast and 2^24 multicast identifiers; 00-00-5E-00-53-00 to 00-00-5E-00-53-FF for documentation", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9542.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 1122, Requirements for Internet Hosts, Communication Layers, R. Braden (ed.), October 1989 (read 2026-11-11): the link layer must tell IP whether a packet arrived as a link-layer broadcast; a host SHOULD silently discard a datagram received by link-layer broadcast without an IP broadcast or multicast destination; a mis-addressed datagram may come from a link-layer broadcast of a unicast datagram; no ICMP errors for broadcast or multicast datagrams, to prevent broadcast storms; the Limited Broadcast address", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 1112, Host Extensions for IP Multicasting, S. Deering, August 1989 (read 2026-11-11): multicast delivered with the same best-efforts reliability as unicast; class D addresses (high-order bits 1110, 224.0.0.0 to 239.255.255.255) for host groups and class E (1111) reserved; an IP unicast address statically bound to a single interface on a single network; low-order 23 bits mapped into 01-00-5E", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1112.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 919, Broadcasting Internet Datagrams, J. Mogul, October 1984 (read 2026-11-11): simple rules proposed for broadcasting Internet datagrams on local networks and for how gateways handle them", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc919.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 1546, Host Anycasting Service, C. Partridge, T. Mendez and W. Milliken (BBN), November 1993 (read 2026-11-11): anycasting is a stateless service with no obligation to deliver successive packets to the same host; UDP can treat an anycast address like a unicast one; TCP is stateful, so the initiator replaces the anycast address with the address that returns the SYN-ACK", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1546.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 826, An Ethernet Address Resolution Protocol, D. Plummer, November 1982 (read 2026-11-11): the request is broadcast to every station on the cable; the reply is sent directly, not broadcast, to the requester", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc826.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 2131, Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol, R. Droms, March 1997 (read 2026-11-11): the BROADCAST flag for clients that cannot receive unicast before configuration; otherwise DHCPOFFER and DHCPACK are unicast to yiaddr and chaddr; DHCPNAK broadcast to 0xffffffff when giaddr is zero; replies through a relay go to giaddr", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2131.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "Cisco, Unicast Flooding in Switched Campus Networks, document 23563, updated 8 February 2016 (read 2026-11-11): three causes of unicast flooding (asymmetric routing, spanning tree topology change notifications shortening the aging time, forwarding table overflow including MAC flooding attacks); fixes: router ARP timeout close to the switch aging time, PortFast, port security, switchport block unicast, unicast flood protection", href: "https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/switches/catalyst-6000-series-switches/23563-143.html" }, // Cisco
      { label: "IETF, RFC 7432, BGP MPLS-Based Ethernet VPN, A. Sajassi (ed.) et al., February 2015 (read 2026-11-11): broadcast, unknown unicast, or multicast (BUM) traffic handled as one class, with split-horizon and ingress replication", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7432.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "Linux kernel documentation, Ethernet Bridging (docs.kernel.org, 7.4.0-rc2) (read 2026-11-11): FDB aging time default 300 seconds; per-port unicast flood of frames with no FDB entry on by default; mcast_to_unicast copies a multicast packet per interested host with a unicast destination MAC, intended for links such as Wi-Fi where unicast is more reliable or efficient", href: "https://docs.kernel.org/networking/bridge.html" }, // Linux kernel documentation
    ], // 12 sources, one object per line
