    sources: [ // every label says what the page says and when it was read (batch 12B)
      { label: "WHATWG, Fetch Living Standard, last updated 19 October 2026 (read 2026-11-12): section 3.3, the CORS protocol, an opt-in layered on HTTP to prevent leaking data from responses behind a firewall and data sent with credentials; a CORS request carries an Origin header; the preflight uses OPTIONS with Access-Control-Request-Method and Access-Control-Request-Headers; response headers Access-Control-Allow-Origin, -Allow-Credentials, -Allow-Methods, -Allow-Headers, -Max-Age (5 seconds by default) and -Expose-Headers; ok status such as 200 or 204; a preflight never includes credentials; the credentials table (no wildcard with include, true is case-sensitive, no trailing slash); the confused deputy warning; side channels such as timing; Vary: Origin; safelisted methods GET, HEAD and POST and the three safelisted content types; it supersedes the Origin semantics of RFC 6454", href: "https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/" }, // WHATWG
      { label: "W3C, Cross-Origin Resource Sharing, Recommendation 16 January 2014, superseded 2 June 2020, editor Anne van Kesteren (read 2026-11-12): a Superseded Recommendation under the W3C and WHATWG memorandum; new implementations should follow the Fetch standard; produced by the WebAppSec and WebApps working groups; earlier versions edited with Brad Porter, Matt Oshry and R. Auburn", href: "https://www.w3.org/TR/2020/SPSD-cors-20200602/" }, // W3C
      { label: "WHATWG, FAQ (read 2026-11-12): the Web Hypertext Application Technology Working Group (WHATWG) is a community evolving the web through standards and tests, founded by individuals of Apple, the Mozilla Foundation and Opera Software in 2004", href: "https://whatwg.org/faq" }, // WHATWG
      { label: "IETF, RFC 6454, The Web Origin Concept, A. Barth, December 2011 (read 2026-11-12): an origin is the scheme, host and port of a URI; two URIs are same-origin only when all three match", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6454.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "MDN Web Docs, Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) guide (read 2026-11-12): fetch() and XMLHttpRequest follow the same-origin policy; preflight examples with Access-Control-Max-Age and Vary: Origin; each browser has a maximum internal value for Max-Age; Set-Cookie ignored when the origin is the wildcard; third-party cookie policies apply", href: "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CORS" }, // MDN Web Docs
      { label: "MDN Web Docs, Access-Control-Max-Age (read 2026-11-12): Firefox caps it at 24 hours (86400 seconds); Chromium caps it at 2 hours (7200 seconds) from version 76 and at 10 minutes before; default 5 seconds", href: "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Access-Control-Max-Age" }, // MDN Web Docs
      { label: "MDN Web Docs, Access-Control-Allow-Origin (read 2026-11-12): a single origin per response; the wildcard with credentials is an error; the value null should not be used, because data: and file: resources and sandboxed documents are serialized as null", href: "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Access-Control-Allow-Origin" }, // MDN Web Docs
      { label: "MITRE, CWE-942, Permissive Cross-domain Policy with Untrusted Domains (read 2026-11-12): a web-client protection policy that includes untrusted domains; mitigations define a restrictive policy and avoid wildcards", href: "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/942.html" }, // MITRE
      { label: "F5, BIG-IP Application Security Manager: Implementations 17.5, Setting Up Cross-Domain Request Enforcement, updated 7 July 2026 (read 2026-11-12): HTML5 Cross-Domain Request Enforcement per URL with the modes Disabled, Remove all CORS headers, Replace CORS headers and Enforce on ASM, the last recommended for maximum security; an Illegal cross-origin request violation when the Origin header is missing or repeated", href: "https://techdocs.f5.com/en-us/bigip-17-5-0/big-ip-asm-implementations/setting-up-cross-domain-request-enforcement.html" }, // F5
    ], // 9 sources, one object per line
