    sources: [ // every label says what the page says and when it was read (11/11/2026, batch 2)
      { label: "IETF (D. Hardt, ed.), RFC 6749, The OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework, October 2012 (read 2026-11-11): limited access to an HTTP service on behalf of a resource owner or on the client's own behalf; obsoletes OAuth 1.0 (RFC 5849) and is not backward compatible; the five problems of sharing passwords with third parties; access token as a string denoting scope, lifetime and other attributes; four roles; confidential and public clients; four grant types; authorization code lifetime of at most 10 minutes RECOMMENDED, never used twice; initial draft edited by David Recordon, later by Eran Hammer, from OAuth 1.0 and OAuth WRAP (edited by Dick Hardt)", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749" }, // IETF (D. Hardt
      { label: "IETF (M. Jones, D. Hardt), RFC 6750, The OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework: Bearer Token Usage, October 2012 (read 2026-11-11): any party in possession of a bearer token can use it, with no proof of possession; bearer tokens SHOULD NOT be passed in page URLs because browsers, servers and other software may not secure URLs in browser history and web server logs", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6750" }, // IETF (M. Jones
      { label: "IETF (E. Hammer-Lahav, ed.), RFC 5849, The OAuth 1.0 Protocol, April 2010 (read 2026-11-11): created by a small community of web developers; stabilized at version 1.0 in October 2007 and revised in June 2009 (Revision A); informational", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5849" }, // IETF (E. Hammer-Lahav
      { label: "IETF (N. Sakimura, ed., J. Bradley, N. Agarwal), RFC 7636, Proof Key for Code Exchange by OAuth Public Clients, September 2015 (read 2026-11-11): the authorization code interception attack, for example through inter-application communication; PKCE pronounced pixy; code_verifier of 43 to 128 unreserved characters; code_challenge = BASE64URL-ENCODE(SHA256(ASCII(code_verifier))); S256 mandatory to implement on the server", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7636" }, // IETF (N. Sakimura
      { label: "IETF (T. Lodderstedt, J. Bradley, A. Labunets, D. Fett), RFC 9700, Best Current Practice for OAuth 2.0 Security, BCP 240, January 2025 (read 2026-11-11): exact string matching of redirect URIs; public clients MUST use PKCE, RECOMMENDED for confidential clients; authorization servers MUST support PKCE; S256 the only method that does not expose the verifier; clients SHOULD NOT use the implicit grant (leakage and replay, no sender-constraining); the resource owner password credentials grant MUST NOT be used; public-client refresh tokens sender-constrained or rotated; mTLS (RFC 8705) or DPoP (RFC 9449); no open redirectors; mix-up attacks", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9700" }, // IETF (T. Lodderstedt
      { label: "IETF (W. Denniss, J. Bradley), RFC 8252, OAuth 2.0 for Native Apps, BCP 212, October 2017 (read 2026-11-11): authorization requests from native apps through external user-agents, primarily the browser, rather than embedded user-agents; loopback interface redirection", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8252" }, // IETF (W. Denniss
      { label: "IETF (W. Denniss, J. Bradley, M. Jones, H. Tschofenig), RFC 8628, OAuth 2.0 Device Authorization Grant, August 2019 (read 2026-11-11): for devices that lack a browser or are input constrained, such as smart TVs, media consoles, digital picture frames and printers", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8628" }, // IETF (W. Denniss
      { label: "IETF (D. Fett, B. Campbell, J. Bradley, T. Lodderstedt, M. Jones, D. Waite), RFC 9449, OAuth 2.0 Demonstrating Proof of Possession (DPoP), September 2023 (read 2026-11-11): sender-constraining OAuth 2.0 tokens with an application-level proof-of-possession mechanism that detects replay of access and refresh tokens", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9449" }, // IETF (D. Fett
      { label: "IETF datatracker, draft-ietf-oauth-v2-1-17, The OAuth 2.1 Authorization Framework (D. Hardt, A. Parecki, T. Lodderstedt), 31 October 2026 (read 2026-11-11): a working group document that would obsolete RFCs 6749 and 6750 if approved; IESG state I-D Exists; working group milestone December 2026 to submit to the IESG", href: "https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-oauth-v2-1/" }, // IETF datatracker
      { label: "OpenID Foundation, OpenID Connect Core 1.0 incorporating errata set 2, 15 December 2023 (read 2026-11-11): a simple identity layer on top of the OAuth 2.0 protocol that lets clients verify the identity of the end-user from the authentication performed by an authorization server; the ID Token", href: "https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-core-1_0.html" }, // OpenID Foundation
      { label: "GitHub, Security alert: Attack campaign involving stolen OAuth user tokens issued to two third-party integrators, 15 April 2022, updated 27 April 2022 (read 2026-11-11): stolen OAuth user tokens issued to Heroku and Travis CI used to download private repositories from dozens of organizations including npm; investigation began 12 April 2022; tokens not obtained through a compromise of GitHub", href: "https://github.blog/news-insights/company-news/security-alert-stolen-oauth-user-tokens/" }, // GitHub
      { label: "F5, BIG-IP APM OAuth Configuration, OAuth Overview, version 21.1 (read 2026-11-11): APM as an OAuth 2.0 client and resource server, as an authorization server granting authorization codes, access tokens and refresh tokens with token introspection, or both, with two virtual servers and separate host names; the OAuth database synchronized across a device group", href: "https://techdocs.f5.com/en-us/bigip-21-1-0/big-ip-access-policy-manager-oauth-configuration/apm-oauth-overview.html" }, // F5
    ], // 12 sources, one object per line
