    sources: [ // every label says what the page says and when it was read (batch 15D)
      { label: "Wikipedia, Year 2000 problem (read 2026-11-12): Bob Bemer's notice in 1958; Peter de Jager's 'Doomsday 2000' in Computerworld in 1993; the acronym attributed to David Eddy, 12 June 1995; the leap-year rule for 2000; 9 September 1999; date expansion, windowing and compression; the 2020 windowing failures; total cost above 300 billion dollars, an analyst firm's figures; the Fort Belvoir satellite ground station, Shika, Sheffield, Port of Santos and Viracopos incidents; the Year 2038 problem at 03:14:07 UTC on 19 January 2038", href: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Year_2000_problem" }, // Wikipedia
      { label: "United States Senate Special Committee on the Year 2000 Technology Problem, Y2K Aftermath, Crisis Averted, Final Committee Report, Summary of Committee Findings, 29 February 2000, GPO (read 2026-11-12): the US spent an estimated 100 billion dollars, 8.5 billion within the federal government; the committee's judgement that the effort was justified; a lower failure rate than predicted for embedded chips; the low level of disruption abroad somewhat surprising, with four possible reasons; remaining risks around 29 February and business cycles; inaccurate or absent IT inventories found during preparation", href: "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-Y1_3-PURL-LPS90964/pdf/GOVPUB-Y1_3-PURL-LPS90964.pdf" }, // United States Senate Special Committee o
      { label: "EBSCO Research Starters, Y2K crisis (read 2026-11-12): six-digit COBOL dates on 80-column cards saving as much as 20 dollars per record; IBM's System/360 of 1964 adopting the two-digit year; Bemer's warnings of 1971 and 1979; de Jager's 1993 estimate of 50 to 75 billion dollars; a worldwide bill of 500 to 600 billion; a third of small American businesses made no preparations; essential services uninterrupted even in sophisticated, largely unprepared countries such as Russia; the 105-year-old sent to kindergarten and the US Naval Observatory's '19100'", href: "https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/computer-science/y2k-crisis" }, // EBSCO Research Starters
      { label: "Slate, 'Were the Y2K Preparations in Vain?', January 2000 (read 2026-11-12): 200 to 500 billion dollars spent over five years; the 91,000-dollar video rental late fee; Italy and Paraguay spent little and appear to have fared as well as the United States; the experts' reply that spending followed reliance on computers; upgrades that would have been warranted anyway; leap-year and billing-cycle problems still possible", href: "https://slate.com/news-and-politics/2000/01/were-the-y2k-preparations-in-vain.html" }, // Slate
      { label: "The Straight Dope, 'Was all that money spent on Y2K wasted?' (read 2026-11-12): remediation estimated at 100 to 600 billion dollars; Italy had plenty of computers and a lagging effort yet no worse problems; embedded-chip failure estimates cut from 2 to 3 per cent to 0.001 per cent by late 1999; no essential date-sensitive systems found on commercial aircraft", href: "https://www.straightdope.com/21343978/was-all-that-money-spent-on-y2k-wasted" }, // The Straight Dope
    ], // 5 sources, one object per line
