    relatedTerms: ["five-tuple", "lacp", "ospf", "link-aggregation", "bfd", "hash", "asymmetric-routing", "anycast", "load-balancing", "bgp", "mpls", "path-mtu-discovery"], // its neighbours, the existing ones first (batch 14A)
    sources: [ // every label says what the page says and when it was read (batch 14A)
      { label: "J. Moy, RFC 2328, OSPF Version 2, April 1998 (read 2026-11-12): sections 1.1, 2.4, 16.1 and 16.8 and footnote 22; equal-cost routes are all discovered and used and traffic is distributed equally among them; a list of next hops per vertex; network vertices chosen before router vertices to find all equal-cost paths; an implementation may keep only a fixed number of routes; strictly, the result is not a tree", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2328.txt" }, // J. Moy
      { label: "J. Haas and A. Fu, RFC 9764, Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) Encapsulated in Large Packets, April 2025 (read 2026-11-12): section 4.5; BFD Control packets may be forwarded only along links that are up; no general-purpose BFD mechanism for exercising all links in a multihop ECMP; a session may be Up while some member links drop traffic", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9764.txt" }, // J. Haas and A. Fu
      { label: "M. Bhatia, M. Chen, S. Boutros, M. Binderberger and J. Haas (eds.), RFC 7130, Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) on Link Aggregation Group (LAG) Interfaces, February 2014 (read 2026-11-12): sections 1 and 2; one micro-BFD session per member link", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7130.txt" }, // M. Bhatia
      { label: "D. Thaler and C. Hopps, RFC 2991, Multipath Issues in Unicast and Multicast Next-Hop Selection, November 2000 (read 2026-11-12): round-robin and random distribution break path MTU discovery, reorder packets into TCP fast retransmit and mislead ping and traceroute; keep a flow on one path; modulo-N, hash-threshold and highest random weight; transport fields are absent from fragments", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2991.txt" }, // D. Thaler and C. Hopps
      { label: "C. Hopps, RFC 2992, Analysis of an Equal-Cost Multi-Path Algorithm, November 2000 (read 2026-11-12): ECMP routes along multiple paths of equal cost identified by next hop; hash-threshold with a CRC16 key and regions of the key space; disruption of 1/N for HRW, between 1/4 and 1/2 for hash-threshold and (N-1)/N for modulo-N", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2992.txt" }, // C. Hopps
      { label: "R. Krishnan, L. Yong, A. Ghanwani, N. So and B. Khasnabish, RFC 7424, Mechanisms for Optimizing Link Aggregation Group (LAG) and Equal-Cost Multipath (ECMP) Component Link Utilization in Networks, January 2015 (read 2026-11-12): hash-based distribution preserves packet order without per-flow state but balances well only when flows are many, small against link capacity and similar; large flows above about 5 per cent of a link, such as IPsec tunnels and storage backups; recognize and place them", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7424.txt" }, // R. Krishnan
      { label: "P. Lapukhov, A. Premji and J. Mitchell (ed.), RFC 7938, Use of BGP for Routing in Large-Scale Data Centers, August 2016 (read 2026-11-12): section 6; ECMP as the fundamental load-sharing mechanism of a Clos fabric; a fan-out of 32 for 64-port devices; paths equal up to and including step (e) of RFC 4271 section 9.1.2.2; ECMP across ASNs; weighted ECMP; consistent hashing at the cost of TCAM; hierarchical ECMP raises the risk of flow polarization", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7938.txt" }, // P. Lapukhov
      { label: "B. Carpenter and S. Amante, RFC 6438, Using the IPv6 Flow Label for Equal Cost Multipath Routing and Link Aggregation in Tunnels, November 2011 (read 2026-11-12): how the flow label's restrictions apply to load balancing by ECMP and link aggregation, particularly for IP-in-IPv6 tunnelled traffic", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6438.txt" }, // B. Carpenter and S. Amante
      { label: "K. Kompella, J. Drake, S. Amante, W. Henderickx and L. Yong, RFC 6790, The Use of Entropy Labels in MPLS Forwarding, November 2012 (read 2026-11-12): improving load balancing across MPLS networks with entropy labels marked by an Entropy Label Indicator; updates RFCs 3031, 3107, 3209 and 5036", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6790.txt" }, // K. Kompella
      { label: "M. Byerly, M. Hite and J. Jaeggli, RFC 7690, Close Encounters of the ICMP Type 2 Kind: Near Misses with ICMPv6 Packet Too Big (PTB), January 2016 (read 2026-11-12): an ICMP error is not guaranteed to hash to the same destination as its TCP or UDP session behind ECMP or anycast, which breaks path MTU discovery; mitigation by hashing on the flow inside the ICMPv6 payload", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7690.txt" }, // M. Byerly
      { label: "Linux kernel documentation, IP Sysctl (read 2026-11-12): fib_multipath_hash_policy, default 0 (Layer 3: source and destination addresses plus the IPv6 flow label), 1 (Layer 4, the standard 5-tuple), 2 (Layer 3 or inner Layer 3), 3 (custom, chosen by fib_multipath_hash_fields); fib_multipath_use_neigh", href: "https://docs.kernel.org/networking/ip-sysctl.html" }, // Linux kernel documentation
      { label: "Fortinet, FortiOS 8.0.0 Administration Guide, Equal cost multi-path (read 2026-11-12): ECMP considered after policy routing; routes need the same destination and costs (distance and priority for static routes) from the same protocol; v4-ecmp-mode source-ip-based by default, weight-based, usage-based and source-dest-ip-based; ecmp-max-paths 1 disables ECMP; ebgp-multipath and ibgp-multipath disabled by default; RFC 4271 conditions f) and g) not evaluated", href: "https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortigate/8.0.0/administration-guide/25967" }, // Fortinet
      { label: "FRRouting, Zebra, documentation (latest) (read 2026-11-12): ECMP as part of normal operations, generally compiled with a 64-way limit; show ip route lists each next hop as a further via line under the prefix; weighted and resilient next-hop groups", href: "https://docs.frrouting.org/en/latest/zebra.html" }, // FRRouting
      { label: "Extreme Networks, SLX-OS 20.1.1 Command Reference, auto-cost reference-bandwidth (OSPFv2) (read 2026-11-12): reference bandwidth 100 Mbps by default; cost is the ratio of the reference bandwidth to the egress interface's bandwidth, rounded up to 1 when lower", href: "https://documentation.extremenetworks.com/slxos/SW/20xx/commands/GUID-4DB44240-5EAF-4B57-A54F-E244409617F5.shtml" }, // Extreme Networks
    ], // 14 sources, one object per line
