    sources: [ // every label says what the page says and when it was read (sweep 9B)
      { label: "F5, BIG-IP DNS: Load Balancing, version 15.0.0, Global Server Load Balancing, updated 7 July 2026 (read 2026-11-12): BIG-IP DNS (formerly GTM); tiered global server load balancing, first the best available pool in a wide IP, then the best available virtual server in that pool; static methods (Drop Packet, Fallback IP, Global Availability, None, Ratio, Return to DNS, Round Robin, Static Persist, Topology) and dynamic methods (Completion Rate, CPU, Hops, Kilobytes/Second, Least Connections, Packet Rate, Quality of Service, Round Trip Time, Virtual Server Score, Virtual Server Capacity) based on metrics collected by the big3d agents in each data centre; preferred, alternate and fallback methods", href: "https://techdocs.f5.com/en-us/bigip-15-0-0/big-ip-dns-load-balancing/global-server-load-balancing.html" }, // F5
      { label: "F5, BIG-IP DNS: Load Balancing, version 15.0.0, Using Topology Load Balancing to Distribute DNS Requests to Specific Resources, updated 7 July 2026 (read 2026-11-12): the order of the topology records affects how the system scores pools and pool members; by default records are sorted by Longest Match, on the request source statement (most specific subnet first), then the destination statement, then the weight; in tmsh the weight is called score", href: "https://techdocs.f5.com/en-us/bigip-15-0-0/big-ip-dns-load-balancing/using-topology-load-balancing-to-distribute-dns-requests-to-specific-resources.html" }, // F5
      { label: "IETF, RFC 1794, DNS Support for Load Balancing (Thomas P. Brisco, Rutgers University), April 1995, informational (read 2026-11-12): round-robin modifications to BIND from around 1986 (Bryan Beecher's Shuffle Address, Marshall Rose's Round Robin); volatile zones need very low TTLs; BIND then treated any TTL under 300 seconds as irrational", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1794.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 7871, Client Subnet in DNS Queries, May 2016, informational (read 2026-11-12): authoritative servers that tailor answers to location use the address of the incoming query, which is the recursive resolver's, not the user's; the option carries part of the client's network and has known operational and privacy shortcomings", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7871.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 4786 (BCP 126), Operation of Anycast Services (J. Abley, K. Lindqvist), December 2006 (read 2026-11-12): anycast is the practice of making a service address available in multiple, discrete, autonomous locations, so that datagrams are routed to one of them", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4786.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "ISC, BIND 9 Administrator Reference Manual, version 9.20.30, Configuration Reference (read 2026-11-12): min-cache-ttl, default 0, cannot exceed 90 seconds; max-cache-ttl, default 604800 seconds (one week)", href: "https://bind9.readthedocs.io/en/stable/reference.html" }, // ISC
      { label: "Amazon Web Services, Amazon Route 53 Developer Guide, Choosing a routing policy (read 2026-11-12): simple, failover (active-passive), geolocation, geoproximity, latency, IP-based, multivalue answer and weighted routing; Route 53 uses EDNS0 to estimate the location of a user", href: "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/routing-policy.html" }, // Amazon Web Services
      { label: "Microsoft Learn, Azure Traffic Manager routing methods (read 2026-11-12): Priority, Weighted, Performance, Geographic, Multivalue and Subnet; clients and the recursive DNS servers they use cache responses, which can skew the distribution when they are few, an effect common to all DNS-based traffic routing systems", href: "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/traffic-manager/traffic-manager-routing-methods" }, // Microsoft Learn
      { label: "NetScaler documentation, Global server load balancing, current release (read 2026-11-12): GSLB sites, GSLB virtual servers and authoritative DNS services; site metrics exchanged with the proprietary Metric Exchange Protocol (MEP); active-active, active-passive and parent-child deployments; static proximity and dynamic round trip time methods", href: "https://docs.netscaler.com/en-us/citrix-adc/current-release/global-server-load-balancing.html" }, // NetScaler documentation
      { label: "F5, tmsh reference, ltm profile dns (read 2026-11-12): enable-gtm decides whether the listener handles queries for wide IP names; edns0-client-subnet-insert adds the client subnet option to queries that lack one", href: "https://clouddocs.f5.com/cli/tmsh-reference/latest/modules/ltm/ltm_profile_dns.html" }, // F5
    ], // 10 sources, one object per line
