    relatedArticles: ["cipher-families", "hybrid-key-exchange-in-tls", "which-cipher-suites-to-use", "diffie-hellman-for-everyone", "quantum-threat-to-cryptography", "bigip-post-quantum-tls", "the-named-tls-attacks"], // the Learn articles that teach it (11/11/2026, batch 2)
    relatedTerms: ["ecdhe", "forward-secrecy", "cipher-suite", "dh", "ecdh", "ml-kem", "post-quantum-cryptography", "logjam", "whitfield-diffie", "martin-hellman", "ike", "tls"], // its neighbours, the existing ones first (11/11/2026, batch 2)
    sources: [ // every label says what the page says and when it was read (11/11/2026, batch 2)
      { label: "IETF (E. Rescorla), RFC 9846, The Transport Layer Security (TLS) Protocol Version 1.3, July 2026, sections 4.2 and 4.3 (read 2026-11-11): obsoletes RFC 8446; static RSA and Diffie-Hellman cipher suites removed and all public-key key exchange forward secret; supported_groups and key_share, client shares in descending preference, HelloRetryRequest when no compatible share was offered; named groups secp256r1, secp384r1, secp521r1, x25519, x448 and ffdhe2048 to ffdhe8192; key exchange with secp256r1 MUST and X25519 SHOULD; psk_ke sends no key_share, psk_dhe_ke does", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9846.html#section-4.2" }, // IETF (E. Rescorla)
      { label: "Whitfield Diffie and Martin Hellman, New Directions in Cryptography, IEEE Transactions on Information Theory IT-22(6), November 1976, manuscript received 3 June 1976 (read 2026-11-11): two users communicate back and forth until they arrive at a key in common, which an eavesdropper must find computationally infeasible to compute; security from the difficulty of computing logarithms over GF(q), Y = alpha^X mod q; Merkle's independent public key distribution system", href: "https://ee.stanford.edu/~hellman/publications/24.pdf" }, // Whitfield Diffie and Martin Hellman
      { label: "Bruce Schneier, Crypto-Gram, The Secret Story of Non-Secret Encryption, 15 May 1998 (read 2026-11-11): in 1974 M. J. Williamson of GCHQ invented a classified algorithm remarkably similar to Diffie-Hellman; GCHQ released the documents on 16 December 1997", href: "https://www.schneier.com/crypto-gram/archives/1998/0515.html" }, // Bruce Schneier
      { label: "IRTF (A. Langley, M. Hamburg, S. Turner), RFC 7748, Elliptic Curves for Security, January 2016 (read 2026-11-11): X25519 at about the 128-bit security level over the prime 2^255 - 19 and X448 at about 224 bits; values encoded as 32 or 56 bytes", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7748" }, // IRTF (A. Langley
      { label: "NIST, SP 800-56A Rev. 3, Recommendation for Pair-Wise Key-Establishment Schemes Using Discrete Logarithm Cryptography, April 2018 (read 2026-11-11): key-establishment schemes based on the discrete logarithm problem over finite fields and elliptic curves, including variations of Diffie-Hellman and MQV; SP 800-56B Rev. 2 and SP 800-56C Rev. 2 listed as companion parts", href: "https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/56/a/r3/final" }, // NIST
      { label: "NIST, FIPS 203, Module-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanism Standard, 13 August 2024 (read 2026-11-11): ML-KEM establishes a shared secret key over a public channel; security related to the Module Learning with Errors problem; believed secure against adversaries with a quantum computer; ML-KEM-512, ML-KEM-768 and ML-KEM-1024 in increasing security strength", href: "https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/203/final" }, // NIST
      { label: "IETF (K. Kwiatkowski, P. Kampanakis, B. E. Westerbaan, D. Stebila), RFC 10024, Post-Quantum Traditional (PQ/T) Hybrid Key Agreement Mechanisms for TLS 1.3, August 2026 (read 2026-11-11): X25519MLKEM768, SecP256r1MLKEM768 and SecP384r1MLKEM1024; hybrid secure as long as one component remains secure; X25519MLKEM768 is 4588 (0x11EC), Recommended Y; client share 1216 bytes (1184 ML-KEM plus 32 X25519), server share 1120 bytes; share order reversed for historical reasons", href: "https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/rfc10024/" }, // IETF (K. Kwiatkowski
      { label: "NIST, IR 8547, Transition to Post-Quantum Cryptography Standards, October 2026 (read 2026-11-11): the harvest now, decrypt later threat; finite-field and elliptic-curve DH and MQV and RSA key establishment at 112 bits or more deprecated after 2030 and disallowed after 2035; migration to ML-KEM", href: "https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ir/8547/final" }, // NIST
      { label: "Adrian, Durumeric, Halderman, Green et al., Weak Diffie-Hellman and the Logjam Attack, weakdh.org, disclosed 20 May 2015 (read 2026-11-11): downgrade of TLS connections to 512-bit export-grade Diffie-Hellman; 8.4% of the Top 1 Million domains initially vulnerable; precomputation lets a nation-state break a 1024-bit prime; the most common 1024-bit primes would expose 18% of Top 1 Million HTTPS domains, 66% of VPN servers and 26% of SSH servers; use 2048-bit or larger groups and elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman; cited at the team's deployment guide, since the project's home page links proof-of-concept demos (changed 12/11/2026)", href: "https://weakdh.org/sysadmin.html" }, // Adrian
      { label: "Google Online Security Blog (D. Adrian, D. Benjamin, B. Beck, D. O'Brien), A new path for Kyber on the web, 13 September 2024 (read 2026-11-11): Chrome 131 switches the hybrid codepoint from Kyber (0x6399) to ML-KEM with X25519 (0x11EC); the final ML-KEM is incompatible with the earlier Kyber; post-quantum shares are too big to send two predictions, and an extra round trip is costly", href: "https://security.googleblog.com/2024/09/a-new-path-for-kyber-on-web.html" }, // Google Online Security Blog (D. Adrian
      { label: "IETF (M. Baushke), RFC 9142, Key Exchange (KEX) Method Updates and Recommendations for Secure Shell (SSH), January 2022, and OpenSSH Release Notes (read 2026-11-11): group1 (1024-bit MODP, about 80 bits) SHOULD NOT, diffie-hellman-group14-sha256 MUST, curve25519-sha256 SHOULD; OpenSSH 9.0 (2022-04-08) sntrup761x25519-sha512 by default, OpenSSH 10.0 (2025-04-09) mlkem768x25519-sha256 by default", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9142" }, // IETF (M. Baushke)
      { label: "IETF (Y. Nir, T. Kivinen, P. Wouters, D. Migault), RFC 8247, Algorithm Implementation Requirements and Usage Guidance for IKEv2, September 2017 (read 2026-11-11): Diffie-Hellman group 14 (2048-bit MODP) MUST, group 19 (256-bit ECP) SHOULD, groups 2 and 5 SHOULD NOT, group 1 (768-bit) MUST NOT", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8247" }, // IETF (Y. Nir
      { label: "IETF (T. Ylonen, C. Lonvick, ed.), RFC 4251, The Secure Shell (SSH) Protocol Architecture, January 2006 (read 2026-11-11): without a verified host key an attacker in the middle runs one session with the client and another with the server", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4251" }, // IETF (T. Ylonen
      { label: "IETF, RFC 10015, Deprecating Obsolete Key Exchange Methods in TLS 1.2 and DTLS 1.2, N. Aviram, July 2026, Proposed Standard (read 2026-11-12): updates RFCs 5246, 8422 and 9325 among others; clients MUST NOT offer and servers MUST NOT select non-ephemeral or ephemeral finite-field DH cipher suites or RSA key exchange suites in (D)TLS 1.2; non-ephemeral ECDH suites SHOULD NOT be offered or selected; IANA has set the Recommended column of all of them to D; FFDHE suites MAY still be offered in (D)TLS 1.3", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc10015" }, // the July 2026 deprecation of RSA and finite-field DH key exchange in TLS 1.2 (follow-up of 12/11)
    ], // 14 sources, one object per line
