    sources: [ // every label says what the page says and when it was read (batch 15B)
      { label: "IETF, RFC 8945 (STD 93), Secret Key Transaction Authentication for DNS (TSIG) (F. Dupont, S. Morris, P. Vixie, D. Eastlake 3rd, O. Gudmundsson, B. Wellington), November 2020 (read 2026-11-12): obsoletes RFC 2845 and 4635 after CVE-2017-3142, CVE-2017-3143 and CVE-2017-11104; meta-RR type 250, never cached, last record of the additional section; 48-bit Time Signed, Fudge (300 seconds recommended), MAC Size, Original ID, Error; BADSIG 16, BADKEY 17, BADTIME 18, BADTRUNC 22 with RCODE 9 NOTAUTH; algorithm table; truncation floor; up to 99 unsigned messages in a TCP transfer; no recommendation for distributing secrets", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8945" }, // IETF
      { label: "RFC Editor, information page for RFC 8945 (read 2026-11-12): Internet Standard (STD 93); obsoletes RFC 2845 and RFC 4635", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc8945" }, // RFC Editor
      { label: "IETF, RFC 2845, Secret Key Transaction Authentication for DNS (TSIG) (P. Vixie, O. Gudmundsson, D. Eastlake 3rd, B. Wellington), May 2000 (read 2026-11-12): the original specification, updating RFC 1035", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2845.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 3645, Generic Security Service Algorithm for Secret Key Transaction Authentication for DNS (GSS-TSIG) (S. Kwan, P. Garg, J. Gilroy, L. Esibov, J. Westhead, Microsoft), October 2003 (read 2026-11-12): a TSIG algorithm based on the GSS-API; updates RFC 2845", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3645.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 2931, DNS Request and Transaction Signatures (SIG(0)s) (D. Eastlake 3rd), September 2000 (read 2026-11-12): public-key transaction signatures, updating RFC 2535", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2931.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 9103, DNS Zone Transfer over TLS, August 2021 (read 2026-11-12): TSIG authorizes zone transfers and protects their integrity but does not offer encryption; XoT adds confidentiality", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9103.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "NVD, CVE-2017-3142 (read 2026-11-12): an attacker who knows a valid TSIG key name may circumvent TSIG authentication of AXFR requests; a server relying solely on TSIG with no ACL could hand a zone to an unauthorized recipient or accept bogus NOTIFY messages", href: "https://services.nvd.nist.gov/rest/json/cves/2.0?cveId=CVE-2017-3142" }, // NVD
      { label: "NVD, CVE-2017-3143 (read 2026-11-12): an attacker who knows a valid TSIG key name may make BIND accept an unauthorized dynamic update; BIND 9.4.0 to 9.11.1-P1 ranges affected", href: "https://services.nvd.nist.gov/rest/json/cves/2.0?cveId=CVE-2017-3143" }, // NVD
      { label: "NVD, CVE-2017-11104, published 8 July 2017 (read 2026-11-12): Knot DNS before 2.4.5 and 2.5.x before 2.5.2 let an attacker with a valid key name and algorithm bypass TSIG when no additional ACL was set, through an improper validity period check", href: "https://services.nvd.nist.gov/rest/json/cves/2.0?cveId=CVE-2017-11104" }, // NVD
      { label: "ISC Knowledge Base, CVE-2017-3143: An attacker may be able to forge a valid TSIG or signature for a dynamic update, posted 29 June 2017 (read 2026-11-12): severity High; credit to Clément Berthaux of Synacktiv", href: "https://kb.isc.org/docs/aa-01503" }, // ISC Knowledge Base
      { label: "ISC, BIND 9 Manual Pages, version 9.20.30 (read 2026-11-12): dig -k keyfile and -y with hmac-md5 as default unless MD5 is disabled, and the warning that -y exposes the secret to ps and shell history; ddns-confgen and tsig-keygen with hmac-sha256 by default; nsupdate -k, -y, -l and -g (GSS-TSIG)", href: "https://bind9.readthedocs.io/en/stable/manpages.html" }, // ISC
      { label: "Microsoft Learn, Dynamic update (Windows Server), dated 13 February 2025 (read 2026-11-12): the DNS Server service allows only secure dynamic updates by default; secure dynamic update negotiates a security context with TKEY records and carries the signature in a TSIG record; updates are applied to Active Directory with the client's credentials", href: "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/dynamic-update" }, // Microsoft Learn
      { label: "F5, BIG-IP DNS Services: Implementations 21.1.0, Configuring a DNS Zone Proxy, updated 7 July 2026 (read 2026-11-12): TSIG keys under DNS, Delivery, Keys, TSIG Key List with a name, algorithm and secret; the BIG-IP validates a client's TSIG-signed transfer request, forwards it unsigned and signs the response", href: "https://techdocs.f5.com/en-us/bigip-21-1-0/big-ip-dns-services-implementations/configuring-a-dns-zone-proxy.html" }, // F5
    ], // 13 sources, one object per line
