    relatedTools: ["dig-output-explainer", "nslookup-output-explainer"], // the tools that compute or decode it (sweep 9B)
    relatedArticles: ["reading-a-dns-answer", "dns-record-types-in-answers", "authoritative-vs-non-authoritative-answers", "nslookup-errors-explained", "dnssec-records-in-dig", "reading-dig-output", "dns-blocklists-and-response-policy"], // the Learn articles that teach it (sweep 9B)
    relatedTerms: ["dns-rcode", "soa", "nsec", "dnssec", "ttl", "dns", "nsec3", "aaaa", "cname", "dns-referral", "recursive-resolver", "rpz"], // its neighbours, the existing ones first (sweep 9B)
    sources: [ // every label says what the page says and when it was read (sweep 9B)
      { label: "IETF, RFC 2308, Negative Caching of DNS Queries (DNS NCACHE) (M. Andrews), March 1998, updates RFC 1034 and RFC 1035 (read 2026-11-12): sections 1, 2.2, 2.2.1, 3, 4 and 5: NODATA as a pseudo RCODE that has to be inferred; NOERROR with no relevant answers; the three types of NODATA response and the referral; type 2 recommended for authoritative servers; the SOA in the authority section with a TTL that is the minimum of MINIMUM and the SOA's own TTL; caching per name, type and class; responses without an SOA should not be cached; limits of one to three hours work well", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2308.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 9824, Compact Denial of Existence in DNSSEC (S. Huque, C. Elmerot, O. Gudmundsson), September 2025, updates RFC 4034 and RFC 4035 (read 2026-11-12): sections 1 to 6, 8, 9 and the appendices: NODATA instead of NXDOMAIN for nonexistent names; the NXNAME type, number 128; the bitmap of an empty non-terminal; the Compact Answers OK flag; operational and security costs; implementations at Cloudflare, NS1, Amazon Route 53 and Knot DNS; code 128 in use since September 2024", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9824.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 6761, Special-Use Domain Names (S. Cheshire, M. Krochmal), February 2013 (read 2026-11-12): section 6.4: users may assume that queries for names under invalid always return NXDOMAIN", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6761.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 9499 (BCP 219), DNS Terminology (P. Hoffman, K. Fujiwara), March 2024 (read 2026-11-12): section 3: the definition of NODATA, and NXRRSET as a mistaken synonym; section 7: empty non-terminals, with the _tcp.example.com example; the names Resource Record Signature and Next Secure", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9499.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 8020, NXDOMAIN: There Really Is Nothing Underneath (S. Bortzmeyer, S. Huque), November 2016 (read 2026-11-12): an NXDOMAIN denies the name and everything under it; the correct response for an empty non-terminal is NODATA; servers that answer NXDOMAIN there are wrong and have always been", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8020.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 2136, Dynamic Updates in the Domain Name System (DNS UPDATE), April 1997 (read 2026-11-12): NXRRSET is RCODE 8, some RRset that ought to exist does not exist", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2136.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 4074 (Y. Morishita, T. Jinmei), on authoritative servers that mishandle queries for IPv6 addresses, May 2005 (read 2026-11-12): the expected answer to an AAAA query at a name with only A records is RCODE 0 with an empty answer section; servers that ignore the query, or return Name Error, Not Implemented or Server Failure, and what each does to the resolver", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4074.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 4035, Protocol Modifications for the DNS Security Extensions, March 2005 (read 2026-11-12): section 3.1.3.1: a No Data response carries the NSEC record of the queried name with its RRSIG, and a single signed NSEC suffices", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4035.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 5155, DNS Security (DNSSEC) Hashed Authenticated Denial of Existence, March 2008 (read 2026-11-12): section 7.2.3: the NSEC3 record that matches the name, without the queried type or CNAME in its type bitmap", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5155.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 9077, NSEC and NSEC3: TTLs and Aggressive Use (P. van Dijk), July 2021 (read 2026-11-12): the TTL of NSEC and NSEC3 records must be the lesser of the SOA MINIMUM field and the SOA's own TTL", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9077.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "IETF, RFC 8198, Aggressive Use of DNSSEC-Validated Cache, July 2017 (read 2026-11-12): a validating resolver can return NXDOMAIN or NODATA from NSEC and NSEC3 records already in its cache", href: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8198.txt" }, // IETF
      { label: "ISC, BIND 9 Administrator Reference Manual, version 9.20.30, Configuration Reference (read 2026-11-12): max-ncache-ttl defaults to 10800 seconds (3 hours) and cannot exceed 7 days; in a response policy zone NODATA is a CNAME whose target is the wildcard top-level domain and NXDOMAIN a CNAME whose target is the root", href: "https://bind9.readthedocs.io/en/stable/reference.html" }, // ISC
      { label: "NLnet Labs, Unbound documentation, unbound.conf(5) (read 2026-11-12): cache-max-negative-ttl applies to NXDOMAIN and NODATA answers, default 3600; harden-below-nxdomain follows RFC 8020, and DNSSEC mandates NOERROR for empty non-terminals", href: "https://unbound.docs.nlnetlabs.nl/en/latest/manpages/unbound.conf.html" }, // NLnet Labs
      { label: "ISC, BIND 9 source code, bin/dig/nslookup.c at tag v9.20.30 (read 2026-11-12): with an empty answer section nslookup prints three asterisks, Can't find, the name and No answer", href: "https://gitlab.isc.org/isc-projects/bind9/-/raw/v9.20.30/bin/dig/nslookup.c" }, // ISC
      { label: "ISC, BIND 9 Manual Pages, version 9.20.30, dig, Return Codes (read 2026-11-12): 0 means a DNS response was received, including NXDOMAIN status; 9 means no reply from server", href: "https://bind9.readthedocs.io/en/stable/manpages.html" }, // ISC
      { label: "Linux man-pages, gethostbyname(3) (read 2026-11-12): h_errno values HOST_NOT_FOUND and NO_DATA (the requested name is valid but does not have an address; another type of request may return an answer), with NO_ADDRESS as a synonym", href: "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man3/gethostbyname.3.html" }, // Linux man-pages
      { label: "Cloudflare blog (Dani Grant), Economical With The Truth: Making DNSSEC Answers Cheap, 24 June 2016 (read 2026-11-12): black lies, an NSEC placed directly on the missing name; negative answers of around 300 bytes", href: "https://blog.cloudflare.com/black-lies/" }, // Cloudflare blog (Dani Grant)
      { label: "Google Public DNS, JSON answer for an invented name under example.com with DNSSEC records requested, 12 November 2026 (read 2026-11-12): Status 0, no answer, the example.com SOA with TTL 1800, and an NSEC at the invented name listing RRSIG, NSEC and TYPE128; the same query through Cloudflare's resolver printed the type as NXNAME; an invented name under invalid returned Status 3 from the root zone", href: "https://dns.google/resolve?name=nonexistent-name-9b.example.com&type=A&do=1" }, // Google Public DNS
    ], // 18 sources, one object per line
